---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/sendlane-alternative';
const title = "Sendlane alternatives: accept continuity or an external handover";
const desc = "Compare a documented Privy/Sendlane continuation with a replacement that can preserve customer permissions, campaign promises and unfinished service work.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Does the Privy acquisition mean Sendlane has shut down?",
    "answer": "The announcement establishes the acquisition, not a shutdown or a universal migration deadline. Request the operating environment, service scope and dates for your account before choosing a replacement."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which alternative should replace the account?",
    "answer": "Ask the current Privy team and external candidates to demonstrate the same customer, event, permission and pending-message cases. Select the arrangement that meets your requirements with an owner for exceptions; no fixed vendor rank answers that."
  },
  {
    "question": "Should SMS move before email?",
    "answer": "Choose an order by dependencies. A shared offer, purchase exit or permission update can make an apparently separate channel unsafe to move first. Release one accepted journey at a time and reconcile both queues before the next release."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/alternatives">← All alternatives</a>
    <p class="badge mt-6">Replacement planning · checked November 25, 2026</p>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{title}</h1>
    <p class="mt-7 text-xl leading-9">{desc}</p>
  </section>
  <article class="container prose-shopify pb-16">
    <h2>Start with the offer you can actually retain</h2>
    <p><a class="link" href="https://www.privy.com/blog/privy-acquires-sendlane">Privy announced the Sendlane acquisition on February 2, 2026</a>, describing email and SMS with hands-on support and a connected-platform direction. That is a reason to investigate continuity, not to infer a shutdown. The announcement does not establish every account's product access, renewal terms or transition date.</p>
    <p>Keeping a supported program can preserve staff knowledge and useful service relationships. Ask for a written continuation proposal alongside any external quote: environment, invoice owner, available workflows, service deliverables and unresolved changes. Use the <a class="link" href="/apps/sendlane">Sendlane account review</a> to inventory dependencies; this page turns that inventory into acceptance work for the chosen destination.</p>
    <h2>Give the destination a customer ledger, not just a list</h2>
    <p>Reconcile customer IDs, changed email addresses, phone normalization, purchase history and segment definitions. Count records by usable channel status rather than treating every exported row as sendable. Keep opt-out and complaint suppressions with their provenance; an email permission is not an SMS permission, and a channel-specific opt-out should retain its actual scope.</p>
    <p>Proposed fixture: a collector changes email, retains a phone and has a pending VIP invitation without a discount code. Import the permitted evidence, then ask the destination operator to identify the same person without merging another household member. Record source ID, destination ID, unresolved fields and the final eligible audience. Missing history goes on an exception list rather than being recreated as consent.</p>
    <h2>Translate campaign events into an acceptance record</h2>
    <p>A city-specific tour campaign needs the venue date and recipient timing; a variant restock needs the requested size and available stock. Neither should become a generic launch blast during translation. For each event, record identity, event time, source, relevant product or location fields and the rule that ends eligibility.</p>
    <p>Rehearse a waitlist of 4,200 requests for 80 pieces as a hypothetical capacity problem. Preserve priority and allocate invitations against current availability; do not promise every recipient stock. Repeat with a duplicate stock event and an order arriving after invitation selection but before dispatch. The accepted result must explain the resulting queue, not merely show that an integration connected. Compare <a class="link" href="/apps/klaviyo">Klaviyo</a> or <a class="link" href="/apps/omnisend">Omnisend</a> using these same cases if consolidating channels is a goal.</p>
    <h2>Reconcile the old queue and the new queue together</h2>
    <p>Export scheduled broadcasts and people waiting inside flows with the next step, due time and offer expiry. For each journey choose continue, retire or rebuild remaining work. Do not restart everyone at the welcome message. Reconcile new purchases, opt-outs and support pauses that arrive after the snapshot before releasing the destination audience.</p>
    <p>Keep one sending owner per job while both accounts remain accessible. Test a late STOP on the old number and a purchase during a delayed cart step; inspect both queues and the reply inbox. Get sender ownership, registration, porting feasibility and reply coverage confirmed for the actual number. If a message is deferred past a shipping deadline, the approved behavior may be to discard it. See <a class="link" href="/guides/quiet-hours">quiet hours</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/email-sms-coordination">channel coordination</a> for the scheduling questions.</p>
    <h2>Accept the service handover separately from software</h2>
    <p>List unfinished drafts, promised campaign builds, design assets, segment repairs and open support issues. Assign each an outgoing owner, incoming owner, due date and acceptance artifact. A vendor saying migration is included does not establish who will revise an offer or answer customer replies next week.</p>
    <p>Have the incoming operator explain a gift-versus-self-purchase split, a subscriber exclusion and an early-access campaign without codes using the actual saved rules. If staff still need ongoing assistance, include it in the contract or staffing plan. Retaining Privy/Sendlane support can be a positive fit when this work is useful; reaching a particular anniversary is not a reason to remove it. For a separate SMS proposal, ask <a class="link" href="/apps/postscript">Postscript</a> or <a class="link" href="/apps/attentive">Attentive</a> to demonstrate the required sender and queue behavior rather than assuming equivalence.</p>
    <h2>Cost the overlap and the calendar you intend to run</h2>
    <p>Request quiet-month and peak-month quotes covering email volume, destination-specific SMS usage, carrier charges, service, implementation and overlap billing. Include staff time for maintaining two systems if email and SMS separate. The <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">pricing worksheet</a> supports this comparison without relying on a historical subscription price.</p>
    <p>Illustrative order economics: $120 before a $12 incentive and $48 product cost leaves $60 before delivery, software, returns and support. If revenue already records the $108 paid, subtract only the $48 product cost at this step. Compare contribution with a suitable holdout before scaling a clearance offer to full-price collectors. Retain inventory-aware scarcity, value segments and a quarterly calendar as planned exercises, not claimed merchant results; use <a class="link" href="/guides/offer-discipline">offer discipline</a> and <a class="link" href="/guides/segment-by-value">value segmentation</a>.</p>
    <h2>Release only the jobs with a named owner and evidence</h2>
    <p>Save the customer reconciliation, event payloads, permission evidence, old/new queue states and service acceptance together. Release a limited eligible journey after its specific exceptions are resolved. There is no universal SMS-first cutover or fixed monthly text allowance: set campaign slots from capture promises, audience expectations and overlapping journeys using <a class="link" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">campaign restraint</a>.</p>
    <p>Record what can be paused and which environment receives new permissions during rollback. Restoring the old account must not replay already completed work or discard new opt-outs. Continue the supported arrangement if it passes these checks; choose an external replacement when an actual operating or commercial gap remains. Review <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">permission records</a> and the <a class="link" href="/guides/tcpa">legal review questions</a> for the markets involved. These are proposed acceptance exercises, not tests performed on vendor accounts.</p>
  </article>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} />
</BaseLayout>
